{
  "id": "servika_roma_song",
  "name": "Servika Roma song (Slovak Romani song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Song repertoire of the Servika Roma, the long-settled Romani communities of Slovakia and of the Czech lands to which many moved after the war, documented from the 1950s above all by Milena Hübschmannová. Slow songs (halgatone gila, from Hungarian hallgató, 'for listening') voice grief, poverty and longing in free, ornamented melody, while csardas dance songs are sung to clapping or to Romani string bands of violin, cimbalom and double bass; Ida Kelarová and Kmeťoband are among the artists who brought the songs to concert and studio. The fiddle card stands for the violin.",
  "recipe": "classic mid-1950s servika roma song, ballad Poetry. melodic bowed song accompaniment fiddle, sustained and staccato hungarian tradition concert pedal cimbalom large frame pedal damper cimbalom, kolstein bass specific bowed song accompaniment double bass. field recording. small-diaphragm pencil condenser, portable field-recorder pre. tape 15ips.",
